<h2>Where should the activity filters go?</h2>
<p class="subtitle">Account activity page. Three placements, all using the real nine event types and a quick way to find failed sign-ins. These are wireframes, so ignore styling.</p>

<div class="options">
  <div class="option" data-choice="a" onclick="toggleSelect(this)">
    <div class="letter">A</div>
    <div class="content">
      <h3>Filter bar above the table</h3>
      <p>The controls are always visible: an event type Select, a date range Select, and a "Failed sign-ins only" checkbox. The result count sits underneath them. It's the simplest option and nothing is hidden, but it takes one row of vertical space.</p>
      <div class="mockup">
        <div class="mockup-header">Account activity</div>
        <div class="mockup-body">
          <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-end">
            <label style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px">Event type<select class="mock-input"><option>All events</option><option>Sign-in failed</option></select></label>
            <label style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px">Date range<select class="mock-input"><option>Last 90 days</option><option>Last 7 days</option></select></label>
            <label style="display:flex;gap:6px;align-items:center"><input type="checkbox"> Failed sign-ins only</label>
          </div>
          <p style="margin:8px 0">Showing 4 of 52 events</p>
          <div class="placeholder">Table: When | Event | Device | Location | IP | Details</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="option" data-choice="b" onclick="toggleSelect(this)">
    <div class="letter">B</div>
    <div class="content">
      <h3>Quick views plus event type</h3>
      <p>A radio group gives four quick views: All, Failed sign-ins, Sign-ins and sign-outs, and Account changes. An event type Select next to it handles anything more specific. This option puts the security case first, though it means two controls that overlap.</p>
      <div class="mockup">
        <div class="mockup-header">Account activity</div>
        <div class="mockup-body">
          <fieldset style="border:none;padding:0;margin:0 0 8px">
            <legend>Show</legend>
            <label><input type="radio" name="qv" checked> All</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="qv"> Failed sign-ins</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="qv"> Sign-ins and sign-outs</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="qv"> Account changes</label>
          </fieldset>
          <label style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;max-width:220px">Event type<select class="mock-input"><option>Any type</option></select></label>
          <p style="margin:8px 0">Showing 4 of 52 events</p>
          <div class="placeholder">Table: When | Event | Device | Location | IP | Details</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="option" data-choice="c" onclick="toggleSelect(this)">
    <div class="letter">C</div>
    <div class="content">
      <h3>Filters button with a popover and active tags</h3>
      <p>A "Filters" button opens a popover that holds the type and date controls. Active filters then show as removable tags above the table. This takes the least space, but failed sign-ins end up one click deeper, and it's more JS and focus handling to get right.</p>
      <div class="mockup">
        <div class="mockup-header">Account activity</div>
        <div class="mockup-body">
          <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">
            <button class="mock-button">Filters</button>
            <span style="border:1px solid currentColor;border-radius:12px;padding:2px 8px">Event: Sign-in failed &times;</span>
            <span style="border:1px solid currentColor;border-radius:12px;padding:2px 8px">Last 7 days &times;</span>
          </div>
          <p style="margin:8px 0">Showing 2 of 52 events</p>
          <div class="placeholder">Table: When | Event | Device | Location | IP | Details</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p class="subtitle">I recommend A. Failed sign-ins are one click away, every control is a plain labelled form field, and the diff stays small.</p>
